  "scenario_id": "sc-042304",
  "overall_assessment": "anomalies",
  "findings": [
    {
      "benefit": "CI",
      "years": [2021, 2022],
      "pattern": "shock",
      "direction": "increase",
      "magnitude": "A/E ~1.27–1.30 (+0.27 to +0.30 over baseline)",
      "confidence": 0.85,
      "evidence": [
        "artifacts/ae_ci_by_year.csv: A/E jumps abruptly from 0.880 (2020) to 1.273 (2021) and 1.295 (2022), then reverts to ~0.94 in 2023–2024.",
        "Empirical year-to-year SD of CI A/E is ~0.15, materially exceeding Poisson expectation (~0.03); therefore Poisson z-scores are invalid, but the concurrent two-year high excursion stands out as a structural shock rather than random noise.",
        "artifacts/ae_death_by_year.csv, ae_tpd_by_year.csv, ae_ip_by_year.csv, and ae_ip_termination.csv all show A/E within ±5% of 1.0 with no sustained moves, confirming the anomaly is isolated to CI."
      ],
      "recommended_action": "Investigate 2021–2022 CI claim drivers (case mix, underwriting guideline changes, severity spikes, or data entry errors). Since experience reverted toward baseline in 2023–2024, treat as a temporary shock; no immediate pricing assumption change is warranted, but monitor subsequent years for recurrence. Perform a quick reserve adequacy check for the 2021–2022 development periods if valuation was impacted."
    }
  ]
}

